Concern Virtual threads (MVC) Reactive (WebFlux) Learning curve low — plain imperative Java high — operators, schedulers, context Debugging / stack traces natural needs <code>checkpoint()</code>, ReactorDebugAgent Libraries everything blocking works (JDBC, JPA) needs non-blocking drivers (R2DBC, reactive Mongo/Redis) Streaming (SSE, WebSocket, infinite streams) possible, awkward first-class Backpressure none built in built in Composition (timeouts, retries, races, fan-out) <code>StructuredTaskScope</code> (preview), manual declarative operators Memory per in-flight request small (virtual stack) smallest Pinning pitfalls <code>synchronized</code> + blocking pinned carriers before JDK 24 n/a <p>Recommendation</p> <ul> <li>Default for request/response CRUD services: Spring MVC + virtual threads.</li> <li>Choose WebFlux for: API gateways, BFFs and aggregators with heavy fan-out, streaming   endpoints (SSE/WebSocket/NDJSON), event-stream processing, and anywhere backpressure matters.</li> <li>They mix: Reactor can run blocking work on virtual threads   (<code>Schedulers.fromExecutorService(Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor())</code>), and with   <code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code> Boot backs <code>boundedElastic</code> with virtual threads.</li> </ul> <pre><code>// Java 21+: a scheduler backed by virtual threads — cheap to block on, unbounded in count
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    Scheduler virtual = Schedulers.fromExecutorService(executor, "virtual");
    String thread = Mono.fromCallable(() -&gt; Thread.currentThread().isVirtual() ? "virtual" : "platform")
            .subscribeOn(virtual)
            .block();
    assertThat(thread).isEqualTo("virtual");
}
// -Dreactor.schedulers.defaultBoundedElasticOnVirtualThreads=true makes boundedElastic() virtual too
</code></pre>

1. What is reactive programming? <p>A declarative style of programming with asynchronous data streams: you describe a pipeline of transformations over values that arrive over time, and the runtime pushes values through it as they become available. Its key properties are: non-blocking execution, composition via operators, errors as signals, and backpressure (the consumer controls the rate).</p> <p>Follow-up: Is reactive faster? Not per request — it scales better under I/O-bound concurrency because threads are never parked waiting.</p> 2. What problem does Spring WebFlux solve compared to Spring MVC? <p>Spring MVC (servlet, thread-per-request) blocks a thread for each in-flight request; throughput is capped by pool size and memory. WebFlux runs on an event loop (Reactor Netty) with a few threads and never blocks, so it handles many more concurrent connections, streams (SSE/WebSocket) naturally and supports backpressure. It is valuable for I/O-heavy, high-concurrency or streaming workloads.</p> 3. What is the difference between <code>Mono</code> and <code>Flux</code>? <p>Both are Reactive Streams <code>Publisher</code>s. <code>Mono&lt;T&gt;</code> emits 0 or 1 element then completes (or errors); <code>Flux&lt;T&gt;</code> emits 0..N elements (possibly infinite). <code>Mono&lt;Void&gt;</code> signals only completion/error. Use <code>Mono</code> for single results (<code>findById</code>, a save, an HTTP response), <code>Flux</code> for collections and streams.</p> 4. What are the four Reactive Streams interfaces? <p><code>Publisher</code> (<code>subscribe</code>), <code>Subscriber</code> (<code>onSubscribe</code>, <code>onNext</code>, <code>onError</code>, <code>onComplete</code>), <code>Subscription</code> (<code>request(n)</code>, <code>cancel()</code>), and <code>Processor</code> (both subscriber and publisher). The signal order is <code>onSubscribe onNext* (onError | onComplete)?</code>, and a publisher may never emit more <code>onNext</code> than requested.</p> 5. What does \"nothing happens until you subscribe\" mean? <p>Building a pipeline (<code>Flux.just(..).map(..)</code>) only assembles a description. Execution starts at subscription, and data flows only after <code>request(n)</code>. In WebFlux, the framework subscribes to the publisher you return. If you create a <code>Mono</code> and drop it, the work never runs — a classic bug (e.g. calling <code>repository.save(x)</code> without returning or chaining it).</p> 6. <code>map</code> vs <code>flatMap</code>? <p><code>map</code> applies a synchronous function <code>T -&gt; R</code>, 1:1. <code>flatMap</code> applies <code>T -&gt; Publisher&lt;R&gt;</code> and subscribes to the inner publishers, merging their results — use it whenever the function itself is asynchronous (DB call, HTTP call). Using <code>map</code> with an async function gives you <code>Mono&lt;Mono&lt;R&gt;&gt;</code> and the inner call never executes.</p> 7. What is the difference between <code>Mono.just</code> and <code>Mono.fromCallable</code>/<code>Mono.defer</code>? <p><code>Mono.just(x)</code> captures a value that has already been computed at assembly time. <code>fromCallable(() -&gt; compute())</code> runs <code>compute()</code> lazily on each subscription; <code>defer(() -&gt; mono)</code> builds the whole publisher lazily. <code>Mono.just(callDatabase())</code> calls the database even if no one subscribes — a bug.</p> 8. How do you handle an empty <code>Mono</code>? <p><code>defaultIfEmpty(value)</code> for a default, <code>switchIfEmpty(otherMono)</code> for a fallback publisher (wrap in <code>Mono.defer</code> if building it is expensive), or <code>switchIfEmpty(Mono.error(new NotFoundException()))</code> to turn absence into an error that an advice maps to 404. <code>hasElement()</code> tests for presence.</p> 9. How are errors handled in Reactor? <p>An exception becomes an <code>onError</code> signal that terminates the sequence and travels downstream. Operators: <code>onErrorReturn</code> (static fallback), <code>onErrorResume</code> (fallback publisher), <code>onErrorMap</code> (translate), <code>onErrorComplete</code> (swallow), <code>doOnError</code> (side effect), <code>retry</code>/<code>retryWhen</code>. Without handling, the error reaches the subscriber (in WebFlux → an error response).</p> 10. What happens if you call <code>block()</code> in a WebFlux controller? <p>It blocks the Netty event-loop thread until the result is available, stalling all other connections on that loop. Reactor detects it on Netty/parallel threads and throws <code>IllegalStateException: block() ... are blocking, which is not supported in thread reactor-http-nio-N</code>. <code>block()</code> is for tests, <code>main</code> and batch code only.</p> 11. How do you combine the results of two independent calls? <p><code>Mono.zip(a, b)</code> subscribes to both concurrently and emits a <code>Tuple2</code> once both have a value (empty if either is empty, error if either errors). <code>a.zipWith(b, combiner)</code> is the instance form. Chaining with <code>flatMap</code> would run them sequentially.</p> 12. What is <code>collectList()</code> and when is it dangerous? <p>It turns a <code>Flux&lt;T&gt;</code> into a <code>Mono&lt;List&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> holding every element in memory. Fine for bounded results (a page); dangerous for large/unbounded streams (OOM, no streaming to the client). Prefer returning the <code>Flux</code> directly.</p> 13. What return types can a WebFlux controller method have? <p><code>Mono&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Flux&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Mono&lt;ResponseEntity&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>ResponseEntity&lt;Mono&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>/<code>ResponseEntity&lt;Flux&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>Mono&lt;Void&gt;</code>, plain values, <code>ServerSentEvent</code> streams, and any type known to <code>ReactiveAdapterRegistry</code> (e.g. <code>CompletableFuture</code>, Kotlin <code>Flow</code>, <code>suspend</code> functions).</p> 14. How do you return 201 Created with a Location header reactively? <p><pre><code>return body.flatMap(repository::save)
        .map(saved -&gt; ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/items/" + saved.id())).body(saved));
</code></pre> The return type is <code>Mono&lt;ResponseEntity&lt;Item&gt;&gt;</code>.</p> 15. What is <code>StepVerifier</code>? <p>Reactor's test utility (<code>reactor-test</code>) that subscribes to a publisher and asserts the exact sequence of signals: <code>expectNext</code>, <code>assertNext</code>, <code>expectNextCount</code>, <code>expectError</code>, <code>verifyComplete</code>. It must end with a <code>verify*()</code> call — otherwise nothing is subscribed and the test asserts nothing.</p> 16. How do you test an endpoint in WebFlux? <p>With <code>WebTestClient</code>: bind to a controller (<code>bindToController</code>), a router (<code>bindToRouterFunction</code>), a slice context (<code>@WebFluxTest</code>) or a running server (<code>@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT)</code>), then <code>.get().uri(...).exchange().expectStatus().isOk().expectBody()...</code>.</p> 17. What server does WebFlux use by default and how many threads? <p>Reactor Netty. Event-loop worker threads default to the number of available CPU cores (minimum 4), named <code>reactor-http-nio-N</code> (or <code>-epoll-</code> on Linux with native transport).</p> 18. What is a cold publisher? <p>One that starts producing per subscriber, from the beginning — e.g. <code>Flux.range</code>, a WebClient call, a repository query. Two subscriptions mean two executions (two HTTP calls). Hot publishers emit regardless of subscribers (e.g. a <code>Sinks.many()</code> or a shared ticker).</p> 19. What does <code>then()</code> do? <p>It ignores all elements and emits only the completion (as <code>Mono&lt;Void&gt;</code>), or with <code>then(other)</code> continues with another publisher after completion. Used to sequence side effects: <code>save(x).then(audit(x)).then(Mono.just("ok"))</code>.</p> 20. Can you use JPA/JDBC with WebFlux? <p>Not directly — they block. Options: switch to R2DBC (or reactive Mongo/Redis); or wrap calls in <code>Mono.fromCallable(...).subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())</code> with a sized pool; or, if most of the service is blocking, use Spring MVC with virtual threads instead.</p> 21. What is backpressure in one sentence? <p>The subscriber signals how many elements it can handle (<code>request(n)</code>), and the publisher must not send more — protecting slow consumers from fast producers.</p> 22. Difference between <code>Flux.concat</code> and <code>Flux.merge</code>? <p><code>concat</code> subscribes to sources one after another and preserves source order; <code>merge</code> subscribes to all at once and interleaves elements by arrival time.</p> 23. How do you log what a reactive pipeline is doing? <p><code>.log()</code> (logs every signal including requests/cancel and the thread), <code>doOnNext</code>/<code>doOnError</code> for targeted logging, and <code>checkpoint("name")</code> to label errors with the assembly location.</p> 24. What is a <code>ServerSentEvent</code> stream? <p>A <code>Flux</code> returned with <code>produces = text/event-stream</code>; WebFlux writes each element as an SSE frame as soon as it is emitted. Browsers consume it with <code>EventSource</code>, which auto-reconnects.</p> 25. Name three things that must never run on an event-loop thread. <p><code>Thread.sleep</code>, blocking I/O (JDBC, <code>RestTemplate</code>, <code>InputStream.read</code>, file I/O), and waiting on futures/locks (<code>future.get()</code>, <code>block()</code>, <code>synchronized</code> waits). Also avoid heavy CPU work (big regexes, crypto on large data) without offloading.</p>

