Operator Equivalent imperative idea <code>onErrorReturn(value)</code> / <code>onErrorReturn(Type.class, value)</code> <code>catch → return default</code> <code>onErrorResume(e -&gt; publisher)</code> <code>catch → call fallback</code> (cache, secondary service) <code>onErrorMap(e -&gt; newException)</code> <code>catch → throw new DomainException(e)</code> <code>onErrorComplete()</code> <code>catch → ignore</code> <code>doOnError(e -&gt; log)</code> <code>catch → log → rethrow</code> <code>doFinally(signal -&gt; ...)</code> <code>finally</code> <pre><code>Mono&lt;String&gt; failing = Mono.error(new IOException("disk"));

StepVerifier.create(failing.onErrorReturn("default")).expectNext("default").verifyComplete();
StepVerifier.create(failing.onErrorReturn(IOException.class, "io-default"))
        .expectNext("io-default").verifyComplete();
StepVerifier.create(failing.onErrorResume(e -&gt; Mono.just("from cache")))
        .expectNext("from cache").verifyComplete();
StepVerifier.create(failing.onErrorComplete()).verifyComplete();     // swallow → empty
StepVerifier.create(failing.onErrorMap(e -&gt; new DownstreamException("storage failed", e)))
        .verifyErrorMatches(e -&gt; e instanceof DownstreamException &amp;&amp; e.getCause() instanceof IOException);
</code></pre>

Cold Hot Data produced per subscriber, from the start independently of subscribers Late subscriber sees everything only what is emitted after it joined (unless replayed) Examples <code>Flux.range</code>, WebClient calls, repository queries UI events, Kafka topic, <code>Sinks.many()</code>, <code>share()</code>d intervals <pre><code>AtomicInteger subscriptions = new AtomicInteger();
Flux&lt;Integer&gt; cold = Flux.range(1, 3).doOnSubscribe(s -&gt; subscriptions.incrementAndGet());

cold.subscribe();
cold.subscribe();
assertThat(subscriptions).hasValue(2);   // each subscriber gets its own execution (e.g. its own HTTP call)
</code></pre>

Operator Behaviour <code>cache()</code> / <code>cache(ttl)</code> subscribe once, replay all (or within TTL) to everyone <code>share()</code> multicast while ≥ 1 subscriber; re-subscribe upstream when they come back <code>publish().autoConnect(n)</code> start when <code>n</code> subscribers arrived; never auto-disconnect <code>publish().refCount(n, grace)</code> start at <code>n</code>, stop after the last leaves (+ grace period) <code>replay(n).autoConnect()</code> like publish, but late subscribers get the last <code>n</code> cacheshareautoConnect <pre><code>AtomicInteger calls = new AtomicInteger();
Mono&lt;String&gt; config = Mono.fromCallable(() -&gt; "config#" + calls.incrementAndGet())
        .cache(Duration.ofMinutes(5));   // compute once, replay to everyone for 5 min

assertThat(config.block()).isEqualTo("config#1");
assertThat(config.block()).isEqualTo("config#1");
assertThat(calls).hasValue(1);
</code></pre> <pre><code>AtomicInteger subscriptions = new AtomicInteger();
Flux&lt;Long&gt; ticks = Flux.interval(Duration.ofMillis(10))
        .doOnSubscribe(s -&gt; subscriptions.incrementAndGet())
        .take(5)
        .share();                  // = publish().refCount(1): one upstream, many subscribers

List&lt;Long&gt; a = new CopyOnWriteArrayList&lt;&gt;();
List&lt;Long&gt; b = new CopyOnWriteArrayList&lt;&gt;();
ticks.subscribe(a::add);
ticks.subscribe(b::add);
ticks.blockLast();

assertThat(subscriptions).hasValue(1);   // upstream subscribed once
</code></pre> <pre><code>Flux&lt;Integer&gt; source = Flux.range(1, 3).publish().autoConnect(2); // start when 2 subscribers arrive
List&lt;Integer&gt; seen = new CopyOnWriteArrayList&lt;&gt;();
source.subscribe(seen::add);
assertThat(seen).isEmpty();          // still waiting for the second subscriber
source.subscribe(seen::add);
assertThat(seen).containsExactly(1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3);
</code></pre>

Type Emits Typical use <code>Mono&lt;T&gt;</code> 0 or 1 element, then complete — or an error a single HTTP response, <code>findById</code>, a save <code>Flux&lt;T&gt;</code> 0..N elements (possibly infinite), then complete — or an error query results, event streams, SSE <code>Mono&lt;Void&gt;</code> no element — only completion or error "done" signals: delete, send, write response <pre><code>Mono&lt;String&gt; one = Mono.just("hello");               // 0..1 element
